<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paracelsus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1493">1493</a>–<a href="/years/1541">1541</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Theophrastus Bombast von Hohenheim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paracelsus, born Theophrastus Bombast von Hohenheim at Einsiedeln in 1493, was a Swiss physician, alchemist, lay theologian and philosopher of the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He studied medicine at the University of Basel and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, took his doctorate at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, and between 1517 and 1524 travelled widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, serving as an army surgeon. A pioneer of the medical revolution of the Renaissance, he stressed observation alongside received wisdom and is credited as the father of toxicology. His Prognostications were studied by Rosicrucians in the 17th century, and the medical movement inspired by his works is known as Paracelsianism. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> in 1541.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paracelsus.jpg" alt="Paracelsus" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Quinten Metsys · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paracelsus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paracelsus was born in Einsiedeln, a village in the Canton of Schwyz, in a house next to a bridge over the Sihl River. His father Wilhelm was a chemist and physician, an illegitimate descendant of the Swabian noble Georg Bombast von Hohenheim. His mother was probably a native of the region and a bondswoman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einsiedeln-abbey">Einsiedeln Abbey</a>, where she had worked as superintendent of the hospital. He often referred to his rustic origins and sometimes used Eremita, 'hermit', as part of his name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Seeking a universal knowledge not found in books, Paracelsus travelled between 1517 and 1524 through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He enlisted as an army surgeon. In 1526 he bought citizenship in Strasbourg, and called to Basel he reportedly cured the printer Johann Frobenius. In 1527 he became city physician in Basel with the privilege of lecturing at its university.</p><p>He then resumed the life of an itinerant physician and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1529, where medical professionals excluded him from practising. In 1541 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and died there on 24 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Paracelsus held that physicians needed solid knowledge of natural science, especially chemistry. He pioneered the use of chemicals and minerals in medicine and probably gave zinc its modern name about 1526. He largely rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> and the theory of humours. In his Third Defence he stated that only the dose determines that a thing is not a poison, which he used to defend his use of inorganic substances. He also practised astrology, and called for humane treatment of the mentally ill, whom he did not regard as possessed by evil spirits.</p><p>Many of his works stayed unpublished in his lifetime; Johannes Huser of Basel edited ten volumes in 1589 to 1591. German Rosicrucians venerated him as a prophet. A 1618 pharmacopeia of the Royal College of Physicians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> included Paracelsian remedies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paracelsus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
